#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Write the count-dependent overview and methodological report.

Run after assemble.py; the substantive critical entries are not generated by
this script. Numbers in both summaries are computed from the delivered data.
"""
from pathlib import Path
import collections
import csv
import json
import re
import sys

D=Path(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv)>1 else Path('.')
TAG='GY1965'
talks,items,problems=[json.loads((D/f'{TAG}_{k}.json').read_text()) for k in ['talks','items','problems']]
bib=(D/f'{TAG}_references.bib').read_text()
keys=re.findall(r'(?m)^@\w+\s*\{\s*([^,\s]+)',bib)
with (D/f'{TAG}_corrections.csv').open(newline='') as f: corrections=list(csv.DictReader(f))
classes=collections.Counter(x['class'] for x in items)
by_record={t['record']:t for t in talks}
by_id={x['id']:x for x in items}
problem_for={o:p['id'] for p in problems for o in p['occurrences']}

overview=r'''\noindent\textbf{Scope and editorial position.}
This chapter covers all 37 catalogue records, 53--89, from September 1965 to
March 1966, in the order of the handwriting. Its @@N@@ mathematical
occurrences include repeated explanations and short interventions. The plan
in record 57, the year heading in record 78 and Kushnirenko's title-only
record 79 are preserved without invented mathematical content.
The supplied 52-page bundle begins with two carry-over pages,
\GSpage{P000088--P000089}, already represented by 1964-50.11--24;
Kohn's new-year talk starts on \GSpage{P000090}. Actual page boundaries
therefore replace the catalogue's overlapping attachment ranges.

\paragraph{The main mathematical lines.}
The most sustained autumn sequence develops the Lefschetz formula for
elliptic complexes: symbols, Hodge decomposition, positive-eigenspace
cancellation, regularized traces and the local fixed-point contribution.
The 1964 Harvard notes of Atiyah and Bott already contained this programme;
the later journal publication does not make Gelfand's exposition a new
1965 theorem \citep{atiyahbott1964notes,atiyahbott1967lefschetz}.
Kazhdan's parallel sequence supplies affine algebraic geometry, coherent
sheaves and derived functors. Gelfand's characteristic-form discussions
interact with Arnold's accounts of singularities, normal forms, canonical
transformations and ideal-fluid motion.

The spring records bring several more direct publication matches.
Gelfand--Kirillov's 1966 paper contains the Weyl-field and growth-dimension
results of record 80. The affine-plane transform constructions of record 82
match the 1967 Gelfand--Graev--Shapiro paper. Lidskii's all-zero zeta
continuation and Gindikin's multiple-time parabolic construction likewise
match precise 1967 results \citep{gelfandkirillov1966corps,gelfand1967planes,
lidskii1967zeros,gindikin1967multitime}. Agranovich's calculus had already
appeared in his 1965 survey \citep{agranovich1965elliptic}. These different
publication histories are kept separate from literal correctness and from
claims of historical priority.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}clrr@{}}
\toprule Class & Meaning & Occurrences & Share\\\midrule
@@CLASS_ROWS@@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\paragraph{What the critical reading changes.}
The apparatus separates a faithful transcription of a mathematical mistake
from a mistaken transcription. Examples of the former include the omitted
logarithm in growth dimension and the two extraneous factors in the top's
cyclic momenta. A direct counterexample shows why the torus intersection
bound needs transversality: a Hamiltonian graph on \(T^2\) can have three
intersections, so the unqualified \(2^2\) claim fails. An ellipsoid likewise
disproves the fixed-direction interpretation of the slice-volume formula.
An actual reading correction is decisive for Gindikin: the scan says
\ru{гиперб. оператор}, a hyperbolic operator, where the supplied text says
pseudo-operator. The original paper then provides an exact restricted
match. Restored hypotheses are labelled as reconstructions throughout.

\paragraph{Questions and a bounded development.}
The problem index follows @@P@@ definite questions, including resolved ones.
There is one class-C occurrence: the request to find all automorphisms of
the Weyl division ring, @@WEYL_PROBLEM@@. Its unrestricted form is not
classified in the verified literature; the search did not locate a later
full solution. The development fixes \(D=Q(A_1(\mathbb C))\) and the
rational subfield \(\mathbb C(x)\). It gives complete proofs of its known
stabilizer normal form, an exact pole-and-residue criterion for inner
rational translations, the resulting translation subgroup in the outer
automorphism group, and finite-group cocycle removal. These are explicitly
restricted answers with no novelty claim. Two parameter runs and an
independent written review check the proofs and computations. The remaining
obstruction is moving the chosen rational subfield; no conclusion about
the whole automorphism group follows. There are no class-D items: the
unidentified fragments lack enough information to define research problems.

\paragraph{Continuities with 1964--65.}
The Weyl-rank question links directly to 1964-38.1, and the final
lattice-gas and thermodynamic-limit lines repeat 1964-3.1--2. The elliptic
sequence develops the language of 1964-28.2--5; the derived-functor
construction continues 1964-49.8. Smirnov's positive-definite-function
fragment is related to 1964-13.1, but its missing centrality and topology
prevent identification of the questions. These are links of different
strength, not assertions that every later talk settles an earlier problem.
The supplied previous index contains no problem identifiers, and its
cumulative bibliography is empty. New problem identifiers and checked
bibliography keys are therefore necessary. All present-status assessments
are dated 9 November 2026.
'''
meanings={'A':'Published soon after by the speaker/Gelfand',
          'B':'Developed by others','C':'Partly developed',
          'D':'Not developed','E':'Already known before the talk',
          'F':'Not identifiable from the notes'}
rows='\n'.join(f"{c} & {meanings[c]} & {classes[c]} & {100*classes[c]/len(items):.1f}\\%\\\\" for c in 'ABCDEF')
for old,new in {'@@N@@':str(len(items)),'@@P@@':str(len(problems)),
                '@@CLASS_ROWS@@':rows,'@@WEYL_PROBLEM@@':problem_for['1965-80.10']}.items():
    overview=overview.replace(old,new)
(D/f'{TAG}_overview.tex').write_text(overview)

def table(headers,rows):
    return '\n'.join(['| '+' | '.join(headers)+' |',
                      '| '+' | '.join(['---']*len(headers))+' |']+
                     ['| '+' | '.join(str(c) for c in row)+' |' for row in rows])

speaker_counts=collections.defaultdict(collections.Counter)
speaker_records=collections.defaultdict(list)
for t in talks:
    name=t['speaker'].split(';')[0]
    speaker_records[name].append(t['record'])
for x in items:
    speaker_counts[by_record[x['record']]['speaker'].split(';')[0]][x['class']]+=1

def broad_area(label):
    a=label.lower()
    if 'noncommutative' in a: return 'Noncommutative algebra'
    if 'integral geometry' in a: return 'Integral geometry'
    if 'algebraic group' in a or 'representation' in a: return 'Algebraic groups and representation theory'
    if any(z in a for z in ['algebraic geometry','sheaf','homological','k-theory','projective geometry']):
        return 'Algebraic geometry, sheaves and K-theory'
    if 'singularit' in a or 'desingular' in a: return 'Singularity theory and local algebra'
    if 'probability' in a or 'statistical' in a: return 'Probability and statistical mechanics'
    if any(z in a for z in ['symplectic','hamilton','poisson','lie group']): return 'Symplectic and Lie geometry'
    if any(z in a for z in ['mechanics','hydrodynamic','fluid','euler','geodesic']): return 'Mechanics and hydrodynamics'
    if 'dynamical' in a or 'bifurcation' in a: return 'Dynamical systems'
    if any(z in a for z in ['entire function','value distribution','complex analysis','several complex']):
        return 'Complex analysis and spectral functions'
    if any(z in a for z in ['elliptic','pseudodifferential','distribution','microlocal','operator theory','index theory','spectral theory']):
        return 'Elliptic, microlocal and spectral analysis'
    if any(z in a for z in ['partial differential','cone semigroup']): return 'Partial differential equations'
    if any(z in a for z in ['characteristic','riemannian','differential geometry','complex geometry','curvature']):
        return 'Differential and complex geometry'
    if 'topology' in a or 'linear algebra' in a: return 'Topology and linear algebra'
    raise ValueError('Unmapped area: '+label)

area_counts=collections.defaultdict(collections.Counter)
area_mapping={}
for x in items:
    a=broad_area(x['area']);area_mapping[x['area']]=a;area_counts[a][x['class']]+=1

class_table=table(['Class','Interpretation','Items','Percent'],
                  [(c,meanings[c],classes[c],f'{100*classes[c]/len(items):.2f}%') for c in 'ABCDEF'])
speaker_table=table(['Catalogued speaker','Records','A','B','C','D','E','F','Total'],
 [(name,len(speaker_records[name]),*[speaker_counts[name][c] for c in 'ABCDEF'],sum(speaker_counts[name].values()))
  for name in sorted(speaker_records)])
area_table=table(['Primary area','A','B','C','D','E','F','Total'],
 [(a,*[area_counts[a][c] for c in 'ABCDEF'],sum(area_counts[a].values())) for a in sorted(area_counts)])
assessment_tables=[]
for field,title in [('literal_correctness','Literal correctness'),('publication_match','Publication match'),
                    ('verification','Verification level'),('development','Development'),('confidence','Confidence')]:
    count=collections.Counter(x[field] for x in items)
    assessment_tables.append('### '+title+'\n\n'+table(['Assessment','Items'],sorted(count.items())))
record_table=table(['Record','Date','Speaker','Source pages','Items'],
 [(t['record'],t['date'] or 'Undated',t['speaker'],t['pages'],sum(x['record']==t['record'] for x in items)) for t in talks])
problem_table=table(['ID','Question','Current occurrences','Assessment'],
 [(p['id'],p['title'],', '.join(o for o in p['occurrences'] if o.startswith('1965-')),
   'Partial; bounded development supplied' if p['id']==problem_for['1965-80.10'] else 'Resolved formulation; qualifications retained')
  for p in problems])

report=f'''# GY1965 research report

**Scope:** the I. M. Gelfand seminar, academic year 1965–66, catalogue records 53–89.  
**Research and present-status date:** 9 November 2026.  
**Delivered:** {len(talks)} records, {len(items)} mathematical occurrences, {len(problems)} indexed questions, {len(keys)} distinct bibliography entries and {len(corrections)} correction/apparatus rows. Every record is represented. The plan, year heading and title-only entry have no fabricated mathematical items.

## 1. Findings and counting conventions

{class_table}

These are **occurrence counts**, not counts of independent theorems, publications or research breakthroughs. The repeated Lefschetz exposition legitimately produces repeated occurrences. Definitions, proof methods, constructions and precise questions are separate kinds of item. Small remarks are separated when they have different hypotheses, correctness or publication histories; a single displayed calculation is not split merely to increase the count.

The dominant class is E. The sustained autumn teaching sequence was already grounded in the Atiyah–Bott Harvard notes of autumn 1964, Serre's 1955 coherent-sheaf paper, classical Hodge theory and established algebra. The 1967 Annals publication of the fixed-point formula cannot be used as evidence of novelty in a 1965 expository talk. Class A captures particularly close later matches to Gelfand–Kirillov, Arnold, the Gelfand–Graev–Shapiro plane-transform paper, Lidskii–Sadovnichii and Gindikin. Class B records later exact or appropriately qualified developments by Boardman, Mather and the relevant geometric literature.

There is one class-C item, the unrestricted Weyl division-ring automorphism question. Its restricted rational-subfield stabilizer is developed completely, with known and newly supplied proofs distinguished from any novelty claim. There are no class-D entries. An underdetermined fragment is F even when an appealing modern problem could be invented from it. Conversely, a false literal assertion is not automatically F: when its intended standard theorem is identifiable, its fate and literal correctness are recorded separately.

### Counts by speaker

{speaker_table}

This table attributes occurrences to the catalogued record, not to every voice on the floor. Thus Gelfand's comment in Borovikov's record stays in that record for counting, while the text identifies the speaker of the comment. Dynin's and Bernstein's separately catalogued interventions retain their own records. The dash is the non-speaker year heading. A plan counts as a record, not as a mathematical occurrence.

### Counts by primary mathematical area

{area_table}

Each occurrence has one primary area for this table; overlapping mathematical connections remain in its prose. The delivered JSON preserves the finer original `area` label. The exact grouping is recorded in `evidence/area_mapping.json`, so the counts can be reproduced or regrouped without changing any mathematical classification.

## 2. Assessment fields, separately counted

{chr(10).join(assessment_tables)}

“Verified” in a bibliography entry means that its URL or DOI was opened. It does **not** imply that the full theorem or proof was read. The item-level verification field and the evidence ledgers preserve that difference. “Read the result” applies to the pertinent theorem or passage, not necessarily every page of a long work. A partial match remains partial even when the later paper itself was read in full.

## 3. Corpus boundaries and source order

The attached catalogue assigns overlapping page ranges that sometimes include the tail of an adjacent record. Source headings and transitions determine the actual ranges in the delivered catalogue. The first two supplied scans, P000088–P000089, continue the prior invariant-subspace discussion already indexed as 1964-50.11–24. They are accounted for in the provenance apparatus, not counted a second time as Kohn's 1965 talk. Kohn begins on P000090 with the new date and speaker heading.

Source order differs from numerical record order in three places. Bernstein's record62 precedes the return to Gelfand in61. S. I. Gelfand66 and Smirnov67 precede Gelfand65. At the end, Gindikin89 precedes the boxed Minlos88 heading. The random-walk/heat-equation sentence appears before that Minlos heading; it is retained with89 and its attribution is explicitly qualified. Catalogue dates are preserved even where notebook order is unusual.

{record_table}

The source authority hierarchy was the scan for a doubtful formula or reading, the Russian diplomatic text for wording, and the English translation for navigation. The Russian and English files were read by page, and all assigned records were inventoried. The late-record scans125–139 were all inspected; critical scans elsewhere were inspected at the locations documented in the correction and evidence files. No hidden text was reconstructed from a speaker's reputation or a later paper.

## 4. Problem index and earlier-year links

{problem_table}

The problem index includes precise resolved questions as well as the partially open one. It does not turn every expository theorem into a new problem. The supplied earlier-year index has247 occurrences but supplies no GP identifiers. Accordingly the new GP identifiers are consecutive in first-occurrence order; earlier item identifiers are linked wherever a definite question recurs. The cumulative bibliography contains no entries, so no existing bibliography key was available to reuse.

The strongest cross-year mathematical identity is the Weyl-rank question1964-38.1, now extended to fraction fields and central parameters. It must not be confused with the fixed-rank Dixmier endomorphism conjecture. The Minlos lines repeat the lattice-gas and thermodynamic-limit material1964-3.1–2. Broader continuities include elliptic-symbol and finiteness material1964-28.2–5, derived functors1964-49.8, and central positive-definite functions1964-13.1. The latter is only a thematic link: Smirnov's1965 fragment omits centrality and the underlying field/topology, so the exact1966 central-character theorem does not retrospectively supply those missing quantifiers.

## 5. Searches that materially changed the answer

1. **Atiyah–Bott chronology.** The opened Russian translation identifies the Harvard notes as autumn1964. Its spectral-regularization sections, together with Bott's November1965 Bourbaki report, establish that the long sequence is an exposition. They also prevent an erroneous correction: entire continuation for the transverse geometric trace is stronger than meromorphic continuation of a general spectral zeta function, and is valid in this special setting.

2. **Smirnov's exact short announcement.** Searching Math-Net by the positive-definite-function and nilpotent-group terminology located the1966 Doklady note,170(3),524–525, received3December1965. The full two-page text was read. Its normalization, centrality and discrete characteristic-zero field are decisive. The matching title alone would have produced an overconfident A classification; the underdetermined notebook question remains F with a precise partial match.

3. **Coherent sheaves and K-theory.** Serre's full FAC paper and Borel–Serre's presentation of Grothendieck's Riemann–Roch theory supply the actual hypotheses. Finite presentation over an arbitrary ring cannot replace coherence; projective and coherent-sheaf Grothendieck groups require a resolution hypothesis for comparison. A missing unnamed ring in record66 remains unresolved even though these standard comparison theorems are known.

4. **Singularity and stability statements.** Boardman's original stratum theorems, Arnold's 1968 exposition, and the Mather material distinguish universal smooth jet strata from their possibly singular inverse images, smooth from topological stability, and formal from convergent or smooth equivalence. Later exact matches do not make every handwritten genericity statement literally correct. The explicit complex-square perturbation is followed as a resolved exercise, with its determinant and three ordinary cusps checked directly.

5. **Curvature-jet realization.** The original question about relations among covariant derivatives of curvature is matched to the metric-jet realization statement, with curvature symmetries, differentiated Bianchi identities and Ricci commutation constraints. The accessed Jentsch version is dated13January2026; this is a source-version date, not an assertion that the underlying theorem originated in2026. The chapter does not silently enlarge the question to a minimal presentation of every invariant ring.

6. **The torus intersection bound.** The graphical proof in the notes establishes the Morse case. The unqualified numerical bound fails without transversality. The later Hamiltonian statements are read with their actual assumptions; an endpoint exact-period condition is not silently asserted equivalent to a compactly supported Hamiltonian isotopy. Floer chronology is distinguished from the later exposition actually read. The explicit three-critical-point function is an independent check of the missing hypothesis.

7. **Gelfand–Kirillov's two growth notions.** The original1966 French paper supplies both the logarithm omitted in the notebook and a different denominator-clearing invariant for division rings. The polynomial-subring PBW count alone cannot prove that the quotient division fields have different ranks. The chapter cites the actual division-ring theorem and keeps its base-field requirement.

8. **Alev–Dumas and the Weyl field.** A publisher preview containing the complete1998 chapter gave the exact rational-subfield stabilizers and finite-group invariant-field theorem. Makar-Limanov's1984 theorem concerns the polynomial algebra. Dumas's2011 discussion and the recent Schwarz paper delimit, rather than solve, the unrestricted division-ring automorphism problem. A separate current-status check also located Lauritzen–Thomsen's November 2026 index-symmetry theorem; the exact corollary was read and its different scope recorded. Search leads from informal discussion were followed to primary sources and are not used as mathematical authority.

9. **Agranovich's calculus.** The1965 survey is a much closer source than generic later pseudodifferential textbooks. It fixes angular regularity thresholds, the omitted multiplication term, mapping orders, parametrix qualifications and index constructions. The latter do not require inventing new open problems from broad algebra remarks.

10. **Affine-plane integral geometry.** The1967 Gelfand–Graev–Shapiro article matches the closed-form range conditions, cycles and inversion coefficient. Restrictions on plane dimension and decay are explicit. A nonzero cycle coefficient is an existence statement, not a claim that all cycles invert the transform.

11. **Lidskii's all-zero zeta function.** The exact1967 Lidskii–Sadovnichii result explains why an entire all-zero continuation does not contradict the pole of the ordinary Riemann zeta function. The branch, multiplicities, removal of a zero at the origin and complete sectorial asymptotics are necessary parts of the match. The positive-zero half-sum is a different function.

12. **Gindikin's damaged operator name.** Independent inspection of P000138–139 reads “hyperbolic,” not “pseudo-.” This leads directly to the1967 Doklady announcement, submitted27April1966: the V-elliptic mapping, hyperbolicity cone and cone-semigroup construction are exact matches once their homogeneity and positivity assumptions are restored.

Access failures remain recorded. For example, a publisher metadata page is not promoted to full-text verification when its PDF timed out. The evidence files give source URLs, theorem/page locators and the actual scope of the match. No failure to retrieve an article is treated as evidence that the mathematics was never developed.

## 6. Corrections and mathematical emendations

The CSV contains {len(corrections)} rows. Its fixed schema has no correction-type field, so the **place** and **evidence** cells say whether a row is a genuine transcription/translation correction, an ambiguity to retain, a mathematical error faithfully copied from the notebook, or a provenance/boundary correction. The last two categories are included for a usable critical apparatus; they do not authorize silently rewriting a diplomatic transcription.

Important distinctions include the correctly normalized factor in the principal symbol of a higher-order differential operator, the incorrect shifted-Laplacian cohomology shortcut, the sign of the nonnegative circle Laplacian, the missing fixed-point Jacobian, missing conjugation in a Hermitian pairing, graded signs in characteristic-form calculations, the top's cyclic-momentum factors, and the missing logarithm in growth dimension. Where the actual ink is unclear, the text says so. A standard theorem is not substituted and represented as a recovered reading.

## 7. Proofs, computations and the second check

The development is deliberately confined to one precise part of one class-C question. Over the complex numbers, it fixes the differential Ore field with[p,x]=1 and its rational subfieldC(x). A formal pseudodifferential-series embedding proves the centralizer ofx isC(x). Restriction to the rational function field then gives the fractional-linear stabilizer normal form. Conjugation fixesx only when the implementing element lies in that centralizer, so a rational translation is inner exactly when its shift is a negative logarithmic derivative of a rational function. Partial fractions turn this into the zero-polynomial-part, simple-pole, integral-residue criterion. The translation image in the outer automorphism group follows by quotienting an explicitly described additive subgroup. Finite-group splitting is a direct average of the translation cocycle.

These proofs state their hypotheses and do not infer a global classification. They distinguish torsion in the outer group from finite order of an actual automorphism, and distinguish preserving a chosen rational subfield from preserving the polynomial Weyl algebra. The finite-group proposition assumes that the group already preserves that rational subfield. No assertion that every finite group can be brought into that stabilizer is supplied.

Two exact runs of `code/check_weyl.py`, with seeds 1965 and 20261109, each passed 60 commutator tests, 60 inverse tests, 40 composition tests, 300 independent monomial-action tests, 2 finite-group checks, 175 PBW counts and 3 logarithmic-derivative implementation checks. Two deliberately incorrect formulas failed, as required: the opposite conjugation sign and an omitted chain-rule factor. The written independent review is in `evidence/development_review.md`. The computation output is retained; it is evidence against algebraic or coding errors, not a proof by sampling of the infinite statements.

Additional critical annotations were checked independently. The torus function sin x + sin y − sin(x+y) has exactly the three stated critical points; the two nonzero points have Hessian determinant 9/4, and the origin has cubic leading term xy(x+y)/2. A cutoff Hamiltonian realizes a small graph of its differential. The top cubic was re-derived by eliminating cyclic velocities from the actual recorded Lagrangian. The boundary-cosine identity was checked through the Jacobian from ordered endpoint pairs to unoriented lines and independently on a circle. The ellipsoid slice ratio depends on ab/c², while Fourier slice and Plancherel give the angularly averaged coefficient 8π². These calculations verify or refute precise readings; the angular average is not attributed to a notebook that does not write it. The supporting scripts and mathematical notes are supplied.

Two further independent reviews cover the early and middle explanatory arguments (`evidence/early_proof_review.md` and `evidence/middle_proof_review.md`). They confirmed the principal proofs and caught a false necessity claim in the weighted fixed-point count: unit local weights suffice, but unequal weights can also sum to the number of points. The corrected entry gives a checked real-projective-line example with weights 1/2 and 3/2. The final assembly also repeats the algebraically closed field hypothesis for classical coordinate rings, uses one consistent column-frame convention for the Chern connection, makes the smooth-division example a nonzero germ, and restricts the conditional viscosity estimate to the regular solution's interval of existence with an explicit matrix norm. These changes affect precision, not the classification counts.

The provenance marks `\\ours` and `\\oursComputed` mean “proved/computed here,” not “new to mathematics.” Known statements are cited and may also receive a complete proof here. The same distinction applies to elementary counterexamples in the critical annotations.

## 8. What the classes miss, and proposed method changes

The A–F scheme records a relation between an occurrence and a publication history, but cannot by itself describe correctness, fidelity, novelty or evidential strength. Four distinctions are indispensable this year. First, a later printed version can reproduce an earlier circulating exposition. Second, a speaker's correct intended theorem can be recorded in a literally false equation. Third, a later theorem can settle a reconstructed restricted question while leaving the notebook's omitted quantifiers genuinely unknown. Fourth, a useful bounded development may simply expose and prove a known normal form rather than produce a new theorem.

The assessment fields already address much of this. For later chapters, add (i) a correction-type enum, (ii) first-circulation and publication dates separately, (iii) source-heading/page-boundary confidence, (iv) an item-level speaker-of-intervention field, and(v) a theorem locator tied to each reference rather than one aggregated verification field. Keep occurrence IDs stable; revising a class should not renumber the archive. Continue to prohibit D on the basis of an unsuccessful first title search.

The supplied builder loaded the problem data but did not render the problem index. The delivered compatible copy adds that index, internal links, reciprocal-ID and sequential-item checks, and a readable standalone A4 driver. Corrections are rendered as wide, stacked entries because their mathematical formulas cannot fit legibly in five narrow columns. Long inline formulas receive display breaks without changing their content. Item titles wrap in a dedicated column beside a consistently aligned class badge, and each item has its own hyperlink destination. The complete DejaVu Serif family is bundled with its license for reproducible Cyrillic, italics and bold type; the style retains system-font fallbacks. A Babel locale fallback handles installations without the Russian language-definition package. Untouched supplied versions are retained under `code/kit_original/`. The seven data files keep the requested schema; the extra `area` field is solely an auditable counting aid. The format text calls them “eight files” but enumerates seven; the separate research report supplies the additional requested file.

The bounded search does not establish exhaustive bibliographic absence. The remaining Weyl question is dated and qualified accordingly. Some established statements have metadata or indirect verification only, and retain lower confidence or partial matching where appropriate. The chapter does not assert that all historical discussion has been recovered, that an indistinct phrase has a unique repair, or that the absence of classD proves there were no unrecorded original ideas.

## 9. Reproduction and contents

The principal seven data files, this report, the generated chapter, standalone driver and compiled PDF are at the archive root. `build_chapter.py` and `gelfand_companion.sty` are the compatible build files. `code/` includes the exact computation scripts, their outputs, frozen assembly inputs and the original kit. `evidence/` contains source verification, the independent review, area grouping and assembly/audit records.

```bash
python build_chapter.py GY1965
python code/validate_chapter.py .
xelatex -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error GY1965_test.tex
bibtex GY1965_test
xelatex -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error GY1965_test.tex
xelatex -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error GY1965_test.tex
```

The symbolic scripts require Python3 and SymPy1.14.0. The chapter build uses XeLaTeX, BibTeX, the standard AMS/fontspec/babel packages and the supplied style. To reconstruct the merged JSON/CSV/bibliography from the frozen tranches, run `python code/assemble.py --parts code/parts --output .`, then `python code/write_summary.py .` and the build commands above. The independent mathematics notes are retained as evidence, not additional class-C/D developments. `evidence/build_validation.md` records the actual successful final build and PDF inspection.
'''
